Relevant columns and why they matter  
• bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips  
  – start_date : timestamp that lets us ① decide “first / last” by time and ② pull the YYYYMM key.  
  – duration_sec : trip length; we convert it to minutes for every metric (first, last, max, min).  
  – start_station_id : lets us tie each trip to a physical station.  

• bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations  
  – station_id : join field to trips.  
  – landmark : city name; filter = ‘San Francisco’ so we only keep SF trips.  

Conditions that will be used  
• bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id  
• bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'  

Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final BigQuery SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows where applicable) that gradually build up to the complete solution.

```sql
-- 1. Description: See what city names (“landmarks”) exist in the stations table.
SELECT DISTINCT `landmark`
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Count how many stations each city has.
SELECT `landmark`, COUNT(*) AS station_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`
GROUP BY `landmark`
ORDER BY station_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Quick look at sample San Francisco trips with joined station data.
SELECT bt.start_date,
       bt.start_station_id,
       bs.name AS station_name,
       bt.duration_sec / 60 AS duration_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
  ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
ORDER BY bt.start_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Show how we’ll derive the YYYYMM key for SF trips.
SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) AS yyyymm,
       bt.start_date,
       bt.duration_sec / 60 AS duration_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
  ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
ORDER BY bt.start_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Inspect June‑2015 trips (lets us eyeball first/last durations).
SELECT bt.start_date,
       bt.duration_sec / 60 AS duration_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
  ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
  AND FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) = '201506'
ORDER BY bt.start_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Monthly lowest & highest durations (no first/last yet).
SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) AS yyyymm,
       MIN(bt.duration_sec / 60) AS min_duration_min,
       MAX(bt.duration_sec / 60) AS max_duration_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
  ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
GROUP BY yyyymm
ORDER BY yyyymm
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Use ROW_NUMBER to pick the first trip of each month.
SELECT *
FROM (
  SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) AS yyyymm,
         bt.start_date,
         bt.duration_sec / 60 AS duration_min,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date))
                            ORDER BY bt.start_date) AS rn
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
    ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
  WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
)
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY yyyymm
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Pull first AND last trip durations in a single CTE.
WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) AS yyyymm,
         bt.start_date,
         bt.duration_sec / 60 AS duration_min,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date))
                            ORDER BY bt.start_date)        AS rn_first,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date))
                            ORDER BY bt.start_date DESC)   AS rn_last
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
    ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
  WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
)
SELECT yyyymm, start_date, duration_min
FROM ranked
WHERE rn_first = 1 OR rn_last = 1
ORDER BY yyyymm, start_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Aggregate month‑wise first/last/min/max using ARRAY_AGG ordering.
SELECT FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) AS yyyymm,
       ARRAY_AGG(bt.duration_sec / 60 ORDER BY bt.start_date ASC  LIMIT 1)[OFFSET(0)] AS first_duration_min,
       ARRAY_AGG(bt.duration_sec / 60 ORDER BY bt.start_date DESC LIMIT 1)[OFFSET(0)] AS last_duration_min,
       MIN(bt.duration_sec / 60) AS lowest_duration_min,
       MAX(bt.duration_sec / 60) AS highest_duration_min
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`  AS bt
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
  ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
GROUP BY yyyymm
ORDER BY yyyymm
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: FINAL ANSWER – monthly first, last, highest & lowest trip durations (minutes) for San Francisco trips.
SELECT
  FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m', DATE(bt.start_date)) AS yyyymm,
  ARRAY_AGG(bt.duration_sec / 60 ORDER BY bt.start_date ASC  LIMIT 1)[OFFSET(0)] AS first_trip_duration_minutes,
  ARRAY_AGG(bt.duration_sec / 60 ORDER BY bt.start_date DESC LIMIT 1)[OFFSET(0)] AS last_trip_duration_minutes,
  MAX(bt.duration_sec / 60) AS highest_trip_duration_minutes,
  MIN(bt.duration_sec / 60) AS lowest_trip_duration_minutes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`   AS bt
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS bs
  ON bt.start_station_id = bs.station_id
WHERE bs.landmark = 'San Francisco'
GROUP BY yyyymm
ORDER BY yyyymm;
```
